r calculations.\n\nSegment $\\text{CD} (\\text{denoted as c})$: This is required.\n\nSegment $\\text{DA} (\\text{denoted as d})$: Identical approach.\n\nSegment $\\text{DB} (\\text{denoted as e})$: Result should follow directly from observations.\n\n$\\text{CD > a, b, c, d}$\n\nWe conclude that the longest line segment in the figure is [b].\n\n**Training Aid:**\nOur objective is to find $\\text{CD}$ (denoted as $\\text{c}$) or $\\text{d unknown}$ part. Due to symmetry and similar crate ratios, these segments are equal thus $c=d$. Hence, the longest line segment is $[b]=\\text{CD}$ and also $[b]=\\text{DA}=d$. Lastly, $[b], \\text{b}= \\text{CE}$ because of the symmetry.\n\nHonestly, $\\text{AD}$ and $\\text{AE}$ are also equal because of the symmetry of the circular rendition.\n\nSo $\\text{CD}$, $\\text{DA}$ are the longest line segments, hence by definition as calculated out for .$\\text{CD >a, b, c, d >=e >f >g} <$. $\\textbf{Answer(s)boxed{\\text{B}}$.}",
